---
import Base from '../../../layouts/PlottedBase.astro';
import NearEnemyChart from '../../../components/NearEnemyChart.astro';
import { pieces } from '../../../data/plotted-pieces';
import { summary } from '../../../data/near-enemies';

const piece = pieces.find((p) => p.slug === 'the-counterfeit-virtues')!;
---

<Base title={`${piece.title} · Plotted`} description={piece.dek}>
  <article class="piece">
    <header class="piece-head" data-reveal>
      <p class="kicker">{piece.kicker}</p>
      <h1>{piece.title}</h1>
      <p class="standfirst measure">{piece.dek}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose measure" data-reveal>
      <p>
        Every virtue has an opposite, and the opposite is rarely the problem.
        Nobody sets out to be cruel and mistakes it for compassion. The trouble
        comes from the impostor that sits right beside the real thing: pity
        wearing compassion's clothes, control passing itself off as care,
        certainty borrowing the authority of conviction. Fifth-century Buddhist
        psychology gave these two failure modes names. The far enemy is the plain
        opposite. The near enemy is the counterfeit, and it is the dangerous one,
        because you can practice it for years while believing you are practicing
        the virtue.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is a claim about distance: the counterfeit is supposed to sit closer
        to the virtue than the opposite does. It is usually offered as wisdom
        literature, which means it is usually offered without evidence. So here is
        a way to check it against numbers that were gathered by people who had
        never heard of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2013 a team of psycholinguists paid roughly 1,800 people to rate 13,915
        English words on how pleasant each one felt, how intense, and how much a
        sense of control it carried. They were building a reference database for
        language research. They were not testing any theory of virtue. Twenty-one
        virtue triads from a working list, resolved to the closest words that
        database contains, can simply be looked up in it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bleed">
      <NearEnemyChart />
    </div>

    <div class="prose measure" data-reveal>
      <p>
        The framework survives the test. In {`${summary.nearCloserCount} of the ${summary.triads}`} triads
        the near enemy scores closer to its virtue than the far enemy does.
        Averaged across the set, the counterfeit sits {`${summary.meanNearDistance}`} points
        from the real thing and the opposite sits {`${summary.meanFarDistance}`}.
        Strangers rating words in isolation, with no
        framework in front of them and no reason to cooperate with one, reproduced
        the geometry the Visuddhimagga described in the fifth century.
      </p>
      <p>
        The top of the chart is where it gets uncomfortable. Hope and optimism are
        separated by 0.27 points on a nine-point scale, which is to say they are
        not separated at all. Empathy and sympathy score identically on sense of
        control and differ by 0.72 on pleasantness. These are the pairs that
        careful writers insist are different in kind, and in the aggregate public
        ear they are close to the same word. That is not a failure of the raters.
        It is the mechanism: a near enemy is hard to catch precisely because the
        language has not bothered to keep them apart.
      </p>
      <p>
        Switch the axis to sense of control and a second pattern shows up. Most
        counterfeits do not feel much less pleasant than the virtue, but they feel
        markedly less powerful. Integrity rates 6.96 for control and rigidity
        rates 4.83. Patience rates 7.18 and indifference 5.64. The near enemy
        keeps the warmth and loses the agency, which is a decent working
        description of what it is like to be stuck in one.
      </p>
      <p>
        One triad refuses to cooperate. Discipline's counterfeit, rigidity, lands
        further from it than plain laziness does, which breaks the pattern. It may
        be that discipline is unusual, or that rigidity is doing double duty here
        as the near enemy of both discipline and integrity, or simply that a
        one-word proxy cannot carry the idea. That is the honest reading of a
        method with real limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those limits are worth stating plainly, because they bound what this can
        show. The lexicon holds single words, and several of these ideas are not
        single words: self-abasement had to become "meek," people-pleasing had to
        become "flattery," self-righteousness had to become "sanctimonious." Each
        substitution loses something, and each one was a judgment call that could
        have been made differently. The ratings measure what English speakers
        associate with a word, not what the state is actually like to live inside.
        And the triads themselves are a working list, not a validated instrument.
      </p>
      <p>
        What the exercise establishes is narrower than the framework's full claim
        and more than nothing. The near enemy is not a poetic device. It is a
        measurable position: close enough to the virtue to be mistaken for it,
        far enough to take you somewhere else. This is the map. The series that
        follows walks the territory one virtue at a time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="piece-foot measure" data-reveal>
      <hr class="rule" />
      <dl class="meta">
        <div><dt>Source</dt><dd><a href={piece.source.url}>{piece.source.name}</a></dd></div>
        <div><dt>License</dt><dd>{piece.source.license}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Method</dt><dd>scripts/plotted/build-near-enemies.py</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Built with</dt><dd>Astro · D3 · hand-set type</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="back"><a href="/plotted">← Back to the gallery</a></p>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* One reading column at --measure; the chart breaks out via the shared `.bleed`
     wrapper. Convention: plotted-tokens.css, matching the other pieces. */
  .piece { max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 var(--space-3); }
  .piece-head { padding: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .piece-head h1 { font-size: var(--step-5); margin: var(--space-2) 0 var(--space-3); max-width: 18ch; }
  .standfirst { font-size: var(--step-2); line-height: 1.35; color: var(--ink-soft); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 400; }
  .prose { margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  .prose p { font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  .prose p:first-child::first-letter {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 3.4em;
    float: left;
    line-height: 0.8;
    padding: 0.08em 0.1em 0 0;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .piece-foot { margin-top: var(--space-5); }
</style>
